> The title attribute must, on getting, run the following algorithm:
> 
> If the root element is an svg element in the SVG namespace, then let value be a concatenation of the data of all the child Text nodes of the first title element in the SVG namespace that is a child of the root element. 
> https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage#document.title

Test-case:

```
data:text/html,<!DOCTYPE html>
<script>
var doc = document.implementation.createDocument("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "not-svg", null);
var root = doc.documentElement;
root.appendChild(doc.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "title"));
root.firstChild.textContent = "svg";
root.appendChild(doc.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml", "title"));
root.lastChild.textContent = "html";
document.documentElement.textContent = doc.title;
</script>
```

Because the root element is not named "svg", IE and Firefox output "html".  Chrome outputs "svg".  The spec requires "html".  Chrome's behavior makes somewhat more sense to me than spec/IE/Firefox.  But if we're going to go with IE/Firefox, then we should really change the setter to also only output an SVG title if the root element is named "svg", because currently it requires that

```
data:text/html,<!DOCTYPE html>
<script>
var doc = document.implementation.createDocument("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "SVG", null);
doc.documentElement.appendChild(doc.createElement("title"));
doc.documentElement.firstChild.textContent = "title1";
doc.title = "title2";
document.documentElement.textContent = doc.title;
</script>
```

output "title1", despite having added an SVG title element.  (IE, the only browser that supports setting title for SVG, outputs the empty string in this case.)
